1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:03,800
It's where America has celebrated victory

2
00:00:03,801 --> 00:00:07,300
and rung in the new year.

3
00:00:10,140 --> 00:00:11,580
It's the entertainment

4
00:00:11,581 --> 00:00:14,960
and the advertising capital of the world.

5
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:23,360
Throughout its history, it has exuded glamour, sleaze, progress, and excitement.

6
00:00:24,300 --> 00:00:27,340
Now, Times Square on Modern Marvels.

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:44,000
Time Square, December 31st, 1999.

8
00:00:45,760 --> 00:00:51,560
An estimated 2 million people are jammed into the crossroads of the world, as it has long been called,

9
00:00:52,080 --> 00:00:55,480
with billions more watching on TV around the world.

10
00:00:56,480 --> 00:00:58,120
It's a national party.

11
00:00:58,121 --> 00:01:03,300
it's hard to imagine taking place on the same scale with the same emotion anywhere else.

12
00:01:03,680 --> 00:01:06,260
There's nothing like this. This is New Year's Eve.

13
00:01:06,820 --> 00:01:12,480
You're witnessing the passing of time, the moment when one year ends and the next year begins.

14
00:01:12,740 --> 00:01:15,672
And we've always said, you know, all we have to do is drop the ball.

15
00:01:15,673 --> 00:01:16,620
That's the main thing.

16
00:01:17,300 --> 00:01:20,080
We don't promote this event. People come.

17
00:01:28,120 --> 00:01:35,000
New Year's Eve may not be promoted, but for the millennium at least, it was produced like a Broadway extravaganza,

18
00:01:35,580 --> 00:01:41,120
complete with pageantry, five tons worth of confetti, and a mid-Manhattan fireworks display.

19
00:01:42,720 --> 00:01:48,440
We fire off pyro from two buildings on either side of the venue, as well as One Time Square,

20
00:01:48,540 --> 00:01:51,080
which is where the ball drop actually happens, down on the bookend.

21
00:01:51,081 --> 00:02:01,120
One Times Square itself is a 300-plus foot building, and we probably go another 150 to 250 feet off of that, depending on the various shots.

22
00:02:01,760 --> 00:02:05,840
Total number of shots, my guess, is probably in the range of 1,000 to 1,500 shots.

23
00:02:06,820 --> 00:02:14,960
With tricky wind currents created by so many high-rises, to say nothing of the huge crowd, the explosives must be launched with extreme care.

24
00:02:14,961 --> 00:02:22,780
Fireworks that burn out long before they hit the ground, called cold burn pyro, make this display possible.

25
00:02:25,020 --> 00:02:32,300
The icon of New Year's Eve in Times Square is, of course, the ball, which has received a glamorous makeover in recent years.

26
00:02:33,160 --> 00:02:43,620
It had been the same ball for about 20, 25 years, operated by a rope pulley coming down with light bulbs around it.

27
00:02:43,621 --> 00:02:56,360
In 1995, we took the opportunity to re-gear the ball and add some lights, add some technical effects that led to where we are now.

28
00:02:57,320 --> 00:03:07,200
Designed by Waterford Crystal, the 1,070-pound ball is bedecked with 504 crystal triangles bolted to a latticed aluminum frame.

29
00:03:07,201 --> 00:03:12,440
12,000 rhinestones and 90 rotating mirrors on the outer skin create a

30
00:03:12,441 --> 00:03:16,860
brilliant light show as the ball descends.

31
00:03:16,861 --> 00:03:22,640
Tonight the ball is dropped by computer and timed to the atomic clock in Colorado.

32
00:03:22,641 --> 00:03:26,440
But for the first 87 years it was lowered by hand.

33
00:03:26,441 --> 00:03:34,240
It was done by six guys. So the men would go up there, hoist the ball up at sunset

34
00:03:34,241 --> 00:03:38,380
and play cards until midnight and then lower it down.

35
00:03:38,880 --> 00:03:43,000
I myself was the timekeeper on New Year's Eve for 18 years.

36
00:03:44,120 --> 00:03:46,280
It was like being in the eye of a hurricane.

37
00:03:46,740 --> 00:03:49,580
All of this noise and excitement was swirling all around,

38
00:03:49,581 --> 00:03:53,020
but where we were up on the tower, it was quiet.

39
00:03:53,800 --> 00:03:58,280
That last minute, while the ball was coming down, seemed to last forever.

40
00:03:58,281 --> 00:04:07,680
There's something about Times Square, this magic X created by the crisscross of Broadway

41
00:04:07,681 --> 00:04:16,160
and 7th Avenue just above 42nd Street that lends itself to spectacle and to crowds.

42
00:04:16,161 --> 00:04:21,400
For nearly a century, Times Square has been one of the most vibrant public spaces in America.

43
00:04:21,401 --> 00:04:29,000
In a very real sense, America's Town Square.

44
00:04:29,001 --> 00:04:32,340
It has also been a place of fantasy and excess.

45
00:04:32,341 --> 00:04:37,340
A kaleidoscopic free zone brimming with every imaginable entertainment.

46
00:04:37,341 --> 00:04:40,220
One of the most evocative locations in the world.

47
00:04:40,221 --> 00:04:46,340
In the jungles of Borneo, you say Times Square or Broadway and there's, and people would

48
00:04:46,341 --> 00:04:47,340
have an idea.

49
00:04:47,341 --> 00:04:53,520
Nobody ever gives their regards to Park Avenue or remembers fondly Wall Street.

50
00:04:54,060 --> 00:04:56,760
People want to come and see it first when they come to New York City.

51
00:04:56,761 --> 00:05:01,860
And it's been portrayed in so many movies, going back to the 20s and 30s,

52
00:05:02,180 --> 00:05:08,220
that it's sort of set in the conscious and subconscious of people all throughout the world as the crossroads of the world.

53
00:05:08,580 --> 00:05:15,920
You've got the glitz, the bad taste, you've got the sense of fun, you've got the audience for it,

54
00:05:15,921 --> 00:05:18,680
and you've got complete aesthetic decontrol.

55
00:05:19,500 --> 00:05:25,080
So this chaotic business environment is one that you either have to take it on its own terms.

56
00:05:25,340 --> 00:05:28,480
You have to really love it for its excesses and its sins.

57
00:05:29,120 --> 00:05:30,720
Times Square is really two places.

58
00:05:31,580 --> 00:05:36,240
One is the place that's made out of bricks and metal,

59
00:05:37,020 --> 00:05:40,900
and the other is the Times Square of myth and image.

60
00:05:41,360 --> 00:05:44,100
The mythical one is the powerful one.

61
00:05:44,101 --> 00:05:52,000
The Times Square of myth and of bricks and metal was born in 1904, the year the New York

62
00:05:52,001 --> 00:05:57,580
Times moved into what had long been known as Longacre Square, building a landmark 24-story

63
00:05:57,581 --> 00:06:02,740
skyscraper just north of 42nd Street at the crossroads of Broadway and 7th Avenue.

64
00:06:03,560 --> 00:06:08,360
The tower was modeled after Giotto's famous 14th century Campanile Tower in Florence.

65
00:06:08,361 --> 00:06:16,080
the times tower gave the square definition and focus it was on one hand a very beautifully

66
00:06:16,081 --> 00:06:22,600
detailed imitation renaissance building but it was also a very modern building it had a triangular

67
00:06:22,601 --> 00:06:26,760
shape like the flat iron building and so it was it was very daring

68
00:06:26,761 --> 00:06:34,320
375 feet high the times tower was the second tallest structure in the city

69
00:06:35,200 --> 00:06:37,960
The tallest, actually, if one counted from the basement up.

70
00:06:39,940 --> 00:06:44,700
Constructed at the same time as the New York subway, the building stretched four floors underground.

71
00:06:45,440 --> 00:06:49,700
The printing presses that turned out the times worked underneath two lines of trains,

72
00:06:49,980 --> 00:06:52,420
which actually passed through the remarkable structure.

73
00:06:53,620 --> 00:06:59,780
The decision to put New York's central subway stop, in which 11 lines of inter-borow traffic converged,

74
00:06:59,781 --> 00:07:02,600
at the cross streets of 42nd Street and Broadway

75
00:07:02,601 --> 00:07:05,900
was also crucial to the development of Times Square.

76
00:07:07,860 --> 00:07:11,080
Hundreds of thousands and eventually over a million workers,

77
00:07:11,300 --> 00:07:14,680
commuters and tourists would flow through the area every day.

78
00:07:16,040 --> 00:07:17,920
The subway made Times Square possible.

79
00:07:18,740 --> 00:07:21,240
That you have democratic mass transit

80
00:07:21,241 --> 00:07:23,560
that until the 1940s cost a nickel

81
00:07:23,561 --> 00:07:25,680
and connected to every part of New York,

82
00:07:26,120 --> 00:07:28,940
to every obscure neighborhood, to every immigrant group.

83
00:07:28,941 --> 00:07:33,720
From its inception, it was the most diverse crowds in New York.

84
00:07:34,720 --> 00:07:38,740
And the diversity of the crowd itself was one of the excitements.

85
00:07:40,440 --> 00:07:45,400
From the start, Times Square seemed tailor-made for big parties and big crowds.

86
00:07:46,520 --> 00:07:51,380
The New York Times threw the first New Year's Eve bash on December 31st, 1904

87
00:07:51,381 --> 00:07:53,900
to celebrate and promote its new home.

88
00:07:53,901 --> 00:08:02,120
We did it, I dare say, as a marketing stunt, for lack of a better term, to bring in the New Year.

89
00:08:02,460 --> 00:08:10,380
And that became really, I guess, the focal point almost in the world for when a New Year comes by our calendar.

90
00:08:11,340 --> 00:08:17,100
The Times was responsible for another attraction that made Times Square an enormously popular gathering spot,

91
00:08:17,440 --> 00:08:22,360
an electronic news display that flashed bulletins from the third floor of the Times Tower.

92
00:08:23,900 --> 00:08:30,020
In an age before TV and radio, this electric headline was the most exciting form of communication around.

93
00:08:30,700 --> 00:08:36,900
Riveting crowds like this throng of men who stood for hours watching a play-by-play of the 1919 World Series.

94
00:08:38,700 --> 00:08:48,993
The Times updated the display in 1928, installing a system of chain-driven letters that switched thousands of incandescent bulbs on and off so that messages appeared

95
00:08:48,994 --> 00:08:50,740
to move around the building.

96
00:08:51,500 --> 00:08:54,740
The zipper, as it was soon called, was a real-time ticker tape

97
00:08:54,741 --> 00:08:58,400
that gave people a sense of witnessing history as it unfolded.

98
00:08:59,400 --> 00:09:02,680
Some of the great moments of the 20th century

99
00:09:02,681 --> 00:09:05,780
have reflected back in photos of people gathering around

100
00:09:05,781 --> 00:09:09,000
to watch election results or polar exploration results.

101
00:09:09,400 --> 00:09:12,400
Did Byrd make it to the South Pole on his airplane and things like that?

102
00:09:13,200 --> 00:09:16,200
So it was a wonderful moment in time.

103
00:09:16,201 --> 00:09:23,780
I think to a certain extent it's one of those things that has given Times Square a sense of community.

104
00:09:24,340 --> 00:09:27,140
It is the one fixed point in a changing age,

105
00:09:27,500 --> 00:09:32,980
and it is the one place that people over the decades have learned to look to find out what's happening,

106
00:09:33,100 --> 00:09:34,780
to connect themselves with the rest of the world.

107
00:09:36,340 --> 00:09:39,100
The zipper has a lot of competition these days.

108
00:09:39,940 --> 00:09:44,120
It's now operated by Dow Jones, which acquired the icon in 1995

109
00:09:44,121 --> 00:09:49,420
and replaced the old bulbs with 235,000 light-emitting diodes,

110
00:09:49,600 --> 00:09:51,420
which are easier to read in daylight

111
00:09:51,421 --> 00:09:54,460
and which can display characters at a faster speed.

112
00:09:55,860 --> 00:09:58,600
Normal display speed is 9 characters a second,

113
00:09:59,020 --> 00:10:02,620
but the zipper zips to 15 characters a second with big news.

114
00:10:03,200 --> 00:10:09,080
There are these days a lot of other digital signboards around here,

115
00:10:09,540 --> 00:10:11,180
but there's only one zipper,

116
00:10:11,181 --> 00:10:15,640
and it's clearly the first and we like to think it's still the best.

117
00:10:15,760 --> 00:10:20,600
It is the place that people think of when they think of getting information from Times Square.

118
00:10:21,940 --> 00:10:26,460
The zipper's most famous message was delivered on May 8, 1945,

119
00:10:27,400 --> 00:10:30,340
setting off Times Square's most emotional mob scene,

120
00:10:31,100 --> 00:10:34,980
a wild celebration that still lingers in the national consciousness

121
00:10:34,981 --> 00:10:38,260
as a defining moment for the nation and the square.

122
00:10:38,261 --> 00:10:44,620
It was already very famous, and we already had the celebrations there for New Year's when the Times put their tower there.

123
00:10:45,160 --> 00:10:55,300
But the thing that probably elevated Times Square to crossroad status was the celebration of the victory in Europe and the victory in Japan.

124
00:10:56,640 --> 00:11:03,420
Joan Lowell, an aspiring actress who had volunteered as a hostess at the Stage Door Canteen, serving soldiers on leave,

125
00:11:03,600 --> 00:11:07,880
was among the two million people in Times Square for the VE Day Euphoria.

126
00:11:08,260 --> 00:11:15,440
That was such a historical moment, it was such a personal moment for everybody that

127
00:11:15,441 --> 00:11:18,360
you wanted to share it with other people.

128
00:11:18,361 --> 00:11:24,020
And I think that's what brought this huge crowd just to celebrate and to know that it's

129
00:11:24,021 --> 00:11:25,440
over.

130
00:11:25,441 --> 00:11:29,380
That the horror and the misery was over.

131
00:11:29,381 --> 00:11:33,900
And it was over in that great celebration of people.

132
00:11:33,901 --> 00:11:35,620
Nobody wanted to go home.

133
00:11:35,621 --> 00:11:37,300
Nobody wanted to stop.

134
00:11:37,301 --> 00:11:39,020
He just wanted to stand there and cheer.

135
00:11:40,800 --> 00:11:45,480
Times Square, of course, is not just famous for its great gatherings and delirious crowds.

136
00:11:46,380 --> 00:11:49,400
It's also the entertainment capital of the world.

137
00:11:50,240 --> 00:11:55,040
Up next, the invention of nightlife and the Great White Way.

138
00:11:56,240 --> 00:12:01,360
An estimated 99 million photographs are taken in Times Square every year.

139
00:12:03,020 --> 00:12:05,440
Times Square will return on Modern Marvels.

140
00:12:05,441 --> 00:12:09,900
We now return to Times Square on Modern Marvels.

141
00:12:13,120 --> 00:12:16,020
Entertainment has always been at the heart of Times Square.

142
00:12:17,740 --> 00:12:19,640
All kinds of entertainment

143
00:12:19,641 --> 00:12:24,180
crammed into this showbiz wonderland for nearly a century.

144
00:12:25,660 --> 00:12:28,640
Because this was the entertainment capital of the United States

145
00:12:28,641 --> 00:12:30,700
in the 19th century, the 20th century,

146
00:12:31,220 --> 00:12:35,320
it's got a large body of our shared heritage in it.

147
00:12:35,440 --> 00:12:46,769
And, you know, from the Dancing Girls of 42nd Street to the Slaughter on 10th Avenue to Frank Sinatra and On the Town, these are all things that we share not just as

148
00:12:46,770 --> 00:12:48,760
New Yorkers but as Americans.

149
00:12:53,500 --> 00:13:03,440
Technically, Times Square is defined by the bow tie that includes 7th Avenue and Broadway and extends from 42nd to 48th Street.

150
00:13:04,400 --> 00:13:08,200
But Times Square, as most people think of it, is a larger area

151
00:13:08,201 --> 00:13:10,840
that encompasses an entertainment metropolis

152
00:13:10,841 --> 00:13:13,340
grouped around the theater district of Broadway.

153
00:13:14,780 --> 00:13:18,260
Since its birth, Times Square has been identified with theater.

154
00:13:19,080 --> 00:13:24,260
But the combination of the subways and theater and entertainment district

155
00:13:24,261 --> 00:13:26,480
pushing its way up Broadway, uptown,

156
00:13:27,020 --> 00:13:33,380
made it a place that could be a center for all kinds of group activities

157
00:13:33,381 --> 00:13:36,600
and in particular, a home for nightlife.

158
00:13:38,120 --> 00:13:41,280
The turn-of-the-century theaters that cropped up on Broadway

159
00:13:41,281 --> 00:13:43,260
and along the numbered side streets

160
00:13:43,261 --> 00:13:46,480
sought to outdo each other in extravagance and splendor.

161
00:13:48,600 --> 00:13:50,260
The Olympia, for example,

162
00:13:50,420 --> 00:13:53,280
was billed as the grandest amusement temple in the world.

163
00:13:54,740 --> 00:13:56,140
In addition to three stages,

164
00:13:56,720 --> 00:13:59,060
it offered bowling alleys, smoking rooms,

165
00:13:59,280 --> 00:14:01,460
a Turkish bath, and a roof garden.

166
00:14:03,380 --> 00:14:09,300
For sheer scale and ambition, nothing compared to the Hippodrome, which opened on Broadway in 1905.

167
00:14:10,640 --> 00:14:18,260
Dubbed New York's gigantic toy, the Hippodrome was the world's largest theater with 5,200 seats.

168
00:14:18,880 --> 00:14:26,460
Over a million bricks and 25,000 incandescent bulbs went into the construction of this entertainment Goliath.

169
00:14:27,340 --> 00:14:30,580
The Hippodrome would have two, three hundred people on stage.

170
00:14:30,581 --> 00:14:37,580
They would recreate the whole Ben-Hur, the whole Ten Commandments, the whole parting of the waters, the whole...

171
00:14:37,581 --> 00:14:43,580
I mean, what they did, how they did it, how they could afford those budgets, but they did it.

172
00:14:43,581 --> 00:14:48,580
By 1914, Times Square was generating a new kind of nightlife.

173
00:14:48,581 --> 00:14:53,580
A nightlife of incredible brightness and round-the-clock flare.

174
00:14:53,581 --> 00:14:59,720
Mobs of New Yorkers and tourists came here to enjoy what became known as the Great White Way.

175
00:15:00,300 --> 00:15:04,460
The shimmering, swanky scene that embraced not only the legitimate theater,

176
00:15:04,840 --> 00:15:08,400
but more populist fare like vaudeville and increasingly movies.

177
00:15:09,420 --> 00:15:15,480
Nowhere was the glamour of going to a movie more glamorous than in the new movie palaces vying for attention.

178
00:15:16,620 --> 00:15:19,080
The movie theater is so-called atmospheric theaters.

179
00:15:19,081 --> 00:15:21,960
They were designed not just to turn off the lights and show a movie,

180
00:15:21,961 --> 00:15:24,620
but everything about it from the experience of arriving,

181
00:15:25,320 --> 00:15:29,680
you'd get popcorn, yes, but the guys serving the popcorn would have white gloves

182
00:15:29,681 --> 00:15:33,380
and fabulous ushers' costumes.

183
00:15:33,840 --> 00:15:38,440
And before the theater went on, there would be lighting effects on the ceilings

184
00:15:38,441 --> 00:15:41,260
and great sound before even stereophonic sound.

185
00:15:41,980 --> 00:15:47,180
Generations of Americans grew up finding this the most exciting experience that they'd ever had.

186
00:15:47,400 --> 00:15:49,780
And that's exactly what you want to come to Times Square for.

187
00:15:49,781 --> 00:15:57,460
If anything, Prohibition in 1919 added to the giddy spirit of Times Square

188
00:15:57,461 --> 00:16:01,940
and introduced a kind of illicit allure that would never really go away.

189
00:16:02,800 --> 00:16:08,620
Times Square becomes this place where you can do illegal things and mix everybody up,

190
00:16:08,760 --> 00:16:13,740
the elite and the working class, because both of them are in revolt against Prohibition,

191
00:16:13,741 --> 00:16:21,600
and change the character of the square from one of being a relatively confined entertainment scene

192
00:16:21,601 --> 00:16:26,340
to being completely wide open because now everybody was defying the law.

193
00:16:27,820 --> 00:16:32,260
Interestingly, the Great White Way of Broadway burned brightest during Prohibition.

194
00:16:34,560 --> 00:16:40,900
In 1927, an incredible 76 theaters competed within a few blocks of each other,

195
00:16:40,901 --> 00:16:44,960
staging an all-time high 264 shows.

196
00:16:46,020 --> 00:16:48,160
But business was good all around.

197
00:16:49,000 --> 00:16:51,980
Before the Depression, it was just amazing.

198
00:16:52,340 --> 00:16:57,280
There were, in the Times Square area, 100 nightclubs or more.

199
00:16:57,480 --> 00:17:00,620
100 nightclubs or more where they would give a show

200
00:17:00,621 --> 00:17:04,280
at 8 o'clock, 9 o'clock, 10 o'clock, 11 o'clock, 12 o'clock.

201
00:17:04,281 --> 00:17:08,460
They would give shows at 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, and 7 a.m.

202
00:17:08,461 --> 00:17:11,060
And they were always sold out, always packed.

203
00:17:12,720 --> 00:17:17,180
The entire community around Times Square was geared to round-the-clock action.

204
00:17:18,400 --> 00:17:21,360
The Actors' Church, known as St. Malachy's, for example,

205
00:17:21,840 --> 00:17:24,460
where fans crowded for Rudolph Valentino's funeral,

206
00:17:25,060 --> 00:17:27,500
and where Douglas Fairbanks married Joan Crawford,

207
00:17:28,100 --> 00:17:31,560
offered mass every hour on the hour from midnight to dawn.

208
00:17:33,260 --> 00:17:36,420
The Wall Street Crash of 1929

209
00:17:36,421 --> 00:17:40,320
brought the first golden age of Times Square to an abrupt end,

210
00:17:40,920 --> 00:17:43,620
radically changing the character and the spirit of the area.

211
00:17:44,860 --> 00:17:48,260
Many of the great movie palaces were converted to grindhouses,

212
00:17:48,820 --> 00:17:53,160
so-called because they ground out the same discount double bill again and again.

213
00:17:55,120 --> 00:17:59,540
Vaudeville was on its last legs, offering cheap government-subsidized fare.

214
00:18:00,600 --> 00:18:04,120
And you didn't always have to buy a ticket to see something strange.

215
00:18:04,121 --> 00:18:06,760
like aerialist Tiny Klein

216
00:18:06,761 --> 00:18:10,440
who slid 20 floors from atop the Edison building

217
00:18:10,441 --> 00:18:12,500
by her teeth.

218
00:18:21,160 --> 00:18:22,580
According to one critic,

219
00:18:22,980 --> 00:18:24,700
the once gayest, white-wayest

220
00:18:24,701 --> 00:18:27,160
and most expensive nightlife street in the world

221
00:18:27,161 --> 00:18:28,820
had turned cut rate.

222
00:18:30,700 --> 00:18:33,520
But the new Times Square was not without its attractions.

223
00:18:34,120 --> 00:18:40,660
None greater than Hubert's Dime Museum and Flea Circus, a mainstay on 42nd Street for 40 years.

224
00:18:41,500 --> 00:18:49,620
My one recollection of that museum, of Hubert's Museum and Gallery, was a gentleman named the Great Waldo.

225
00:18:50,400 --> 00:18:58,620
And his performance, done every hour across the board, every hour on the hour, was to put a big fluorescent light in his throat all the way down his stomach.

226
00:18:58,621 --> 00:19:02,420
and you would see the different colors inside the lining of his stomach.

227
00:19:02,640 --> 00:19:04,320
You would see it in a fairly dark room.

228
00:19:04,780 --> 00:19:06,680
It would glow, it would illuminate with the colors.

229
00:19:06,800 --> 00:19:08,300
And one day, I wasn't in the audience this day,

230
00:19:08,320 --> 00:19:12,720
but one day the fluorescent light exploded inside the man's body,

231
00:19:12,800 --> 00:19:14,080
inside his stomach, and he died.

232
00:19:14,360 --> 00:19:15,540
That was the end of the great Waldo.

233
00:19:16,680 --> 00:19:19,220
And that's what happened.

234
00:19:19,340 --> 00:19:20,180
It was just pathetic.

235
00:19:20,181 --> 00:19:21,440
I don't know what to say.

236
00:19:21,640 --> 00:19:22,780
I miss it.

237
00:19:22,781 --> 00:19:30,420
Another form of nightlife that brought notoriety and business into 30s Times Square was burlesque.

238
00:19:32,300 --> 00:19:37,920
By 1937, no less than five burlesque houses were operating along the bow tie,

239
00:19:38,640 --> 00:19:41,760
giving Times Square a new image that would stick for decades.

240
00:19:43,840 --> 00:19:49,860
World War II, which brought hundreds of thousands of soldiers on leave into the square, cemented that image.

241
00:19:50,720 --> 00:19:52,600
These guys came off their ships.

242
00:19:52,760 --> 00:19:53,620
They were on leave.

243
00:19:53,720 --> 00:19:55,000
They'd just come back from war.

244
00:19:55,140 --> 00:19:57,360
They were on their way to be shipped out and maybe die.

245
00:19:57,500 --> 00:20:00,120
So they were extremely willing to have a good time,

246
00:20:00,260 --> 00:20:02,420
and they were ready to spend anything that was in their pocket.

247
00:20:02,960 --> 00:20:07,300
As a result, there was a booming sex industry going on here.

248
00:20:07,400 --> 00:20:08,840
There was a sex industry before,

249
00:20:09,040 --> 00:20:11,540
but I really, really expanded to serve all this.

250
00:20:12,300 --> 00:20:14,600
In the 1950s and 60s,

251
00:20:14,601 --> 00:20:17,680
the sex industry began to dominate Times Square.

252
00:20:18,580 --> 00:20:22,580
Demolition of such landmarks as the Astor Hotel and the Paramount Theater

253
00:20:22,581 --> 00:20:26,020
added to the sense that the area's glory years were over.

254
00:20:27,340 --> 00:20:29,980
Times Square was still full of energy and allure,

255
00:20:30,520 --> 00:20:33,280
but of an increasingly seamy, steamy kind.

256
00:20:34,360 --> 00:20:38,260
One constant through all the changes was the incredible billboards and signs

257
00:20:38,261 --> 00:20:40,920
that have always jostled for attention on Times Square.

258
00:20:41,620 --> 00:20:45,420
Up next, the golden age of Times Square signs.

259
00:20:47,680 --> 00:20:54,680
In 1901, no less than 93 brothels dotted the area around 42nd Street and Broadway,

260
00:20:54,681 --> 00:20:56,680
when it was still known as Longacre Square.

261
00:20:56,681 --> 00:20:59,680
Times Square will return on Modern Marvels.

262
00:20:59,681 --> 00:21:04,680
We now return to Times Square on Modern Marvels.

263
00:21:08,680 --> 00:21:12,680
It has been said that lights are the essence of Times Square.

264
00:21:12,681 --> 00:21:16,680
Certainly, lights are the essence of the electrified billboards and displays.

265
00:21:16,681 --> 00:21:22,180
and displays, spectaculars as they are called by the people who make them, that fight for

266
00:21:22,181 --> 00:21:27,180
attention in one of the most concentrated and expensive advertising spaces in the world.

267
00:21:27,181 --> 00:21:32,440
There is no other show on earth like the visual landscape that you see in Times Square

268
00:21:32,441 --> 00:21:37,460
with these huge spectacular signs that are heaving and breathing light and information

269
00:21:37,461 --> 00:21:42,720
and entertainment and visual things that turn people on.

270
00:21:42,721 --> 00:21:48,780
These giant electrical displays are the only form of advertising that people actively seek

271
00:21:48,781 --> 00:21:50,480
out for viewing.

272
00:21:50,481 --> 00:21:55,040
Otherwise people just turn the page or click on an advertisement.

273
00:21:55,041 --> 00:22:03,260
But these big displays are attractions in and of themselves.

274
00:22:03,261 --> 00:22:07,800
Excessive advertising has always found a home in Times Square, as this turn of the century

275
00:22:07,801 --> 00:22:11,600
photo suggests.

276
00:22:11,601 --> 00:22:17,640
By 1907, the ads were so unruly that one New York Daily launched a crusade to, quote,

277
00:22:17,980 --> 00:22:19,680
end the sign evil, unquote.

278
00:22:20,740 --> 00:22:27,560
But the screaming commercial future of Times Square was fixed in 1916, when the city, after

279
00:22:27,561 --> 00:22:33,300
banning signs on Ritzy Fifth Avenue, lifted all regulations concerning signage in Times

280
00:22:33,301 --> 00:22:34,001
Square.

281
00:22:34,180 --> 00:22:40,600
The first wave of Times Square spectaculars, huge but delicate and creative signs, were

282
00:22:40,601 --> 00:22:42,840
made entirely out of incandescent light bulbs.

283
00:22:44,120 --> 00:22:46,040
They were beautiful animations.

284
00:22:47,120 --> 00:22:55,180
Dancing girls and wire walkers and eagles and birds and boys, boxing,

285
00:22:55,920 --> 00:22:59,320
all made out of incandescent lamps with overlays.

286
00:23:00,060 --> 00:23:01,640
Fine, fine art.

287
00:23:02,120 --> 00:23:07,260
The Corticelli kitten is one of my all-time favorite signs.

288
00:23:07,261 --> 00:23:16,080
the incredible technological feat of getting an animated kitten to play with a ball of yarn.

289
00:23:16,420 --> 00:23:18,500
The kitten would get all tangled up in the yarn,

290
00:23:19,200 --> 00:23:22,620
and the message was that the Quarticelli thread does not tangle.

291
00:23:23,360 --> 00:23:27,720
One of my favorite old signs was an ad for Clico Club ginger ale

292
00:23:27,721 --> 00:23:33,840
because it had Eskimo boys pulling a sled with a great huge bottle of ginger ale on it,

293
00:23:33,841 --> 00:23:38,620
and behind a pattern of colored lights, which were the aurora borealis.

294
00:23:38,621 --> 00:23:42,440
When you're in the city, all the lights in the city really obliterate the sky,

295
00:23:42,540 --> 00:23:46,860
so you no longer have an experience of stars and constellations.

296
00:23:47,280 --> 00:23:53,760
And here we go, and we've got this marvelous little close-up on a substitute sky

297
00:23:53,761 --> 00:23:56,200
that's doing its own little stage drama.

298
00:23:56,201 --> 00:24:08,460
the introduction of neon in the 1920s brought a jazzy look to times square signs

299
00:24:08,461 --> 00:24:10,860
which became more colorful and dynamic

300
00:24:10,861 --> 00:24:18,960
not even world war ii and a band on lights would dim the billboard show

301
00:24:18,961 --> 00:24:25,800
indeed the most famous spectacular of them all the smoking camel sign which blew six foot

302
00:24:25,801 --> 00:24:31,920
smoke rings above Broadway and 43rd Street for 30 years was designed to attract attention without

303
00:24:31,921 --> 00:24:33,052
using light.

304
00:24:33,053 --> 00:24:40,880
What we did was we bought steam from Con Edison and captured the steam in a chamber

305
00:24:40,881 --> 00:24:42,368
behind the sign.

306
00:24:42,369 --> 00:24:49,620
The steam would build up and in the back of the chamber was a diaphragm with a

307
00:24:49,621 --> 00:24:50,960
kind of punching thing.

308
00:24:51,680 --> 00:24:53,500
So it would periodically,

309
00:24:53,501 --> 00:24:54,560
every three seconds,

310
00:24:55,300 --> 00:24:57,020
punch this diaphragm,

311
00:24:57,380 --> 00:24:58,640
forcing the steam out

312
00:24:58,641 --> 00:24:59,600
through a small aperture.

313
00:25:00,500 --> 00:25:01,800
And that's how you blow a smoke ring.

314
00:25:02,380 --> 00:25:04,800
So on days that were not very windy,

315
00:25:05,540 --> 00:25:07,300
the smoke ring would maintain

316
00:25:07,301 --> 00:25:08,620
its integrity all the way

317
00:25:08,621 --> 00:25:09,340
across the street.

318
00:25:12,660 --> 00:25:14,520
The end of the war stimulated

319
00:25:14,521 --> 00:25:16,140
a new generation of playful,

320
00:25:16,320 --> 00:25:17,160
inventive signs,

321
00:25:17,620 --> 00:25:19,600
like this one for Kleenex.

322
00:25:19,620 --> 00:25:22,600
in which little Lulu plucks tissue from a box.

323
00:25:23,480 --> 00:25:28,280
Or this neon masterpiece advertising slips that won't ride up a woman's leg.

324
00:25:31,200 --> 00:25:36,860
The most spectacular spectacular of its time was this Bond clothing sign

325
00:25:36,861 --> 00:25:41,020
featuring a pair of 65-foot statues dressed in neon togas

326
00:25:41,021 --> 00:25:45,720
standing astride a five-story high, 220-foot-wide waterfall.

327
00:25:45,721 --> 00:25:54,080
The waterfall continuously cycled 10,000 gallons of water and 3,000 gallons of antifreeze.

328
00:25:55,080 --> 00:25:59,120
Years later, Pepsi took over this sign and kept the waterfall.

329
00:26:01,220 --> 00:26:06,580
Hyperactive displays that flashed a hypnotic sequence of patterns also emerged after the war,

330
00:26:07,000 --> 00:26:09,400
like this frantic blinker for Canadian Club.

331
00:26:10,700 --> 00:26:15,360
The 50s was a great period because the level of creativity was so high.

332
00:26:15,720 --> 00:26:24,080
The Budweiser sign with the animated Clydesdale horses and the flying A in Eagle.

333
00:26:24,081 --> 00:26:31,820
In order to make that flying A in Eagle, we borrowed a live Eagle and photographed it in motion

334
00:26:31,821 --> 00:26:37,660
and then broke the motion down into six stages of wings flying.

335
00:26:38,800 --> 00:26:44,000
But no such detailed computations went into the signs of the 1960s and 70s.

336
00:26:44,001 --> 00:26:52,000
As the area slipped into depression and sleaze, advertisers for the first time in history stopped advertising in Times Square.

337
00:26:52,760 --> 00:26:55,600
You couldn't give away space in Times Square.

338
00:26:56,260 --> 00:26:58,340
There was nothing happening.

339
00:26:58,960 --> 00:27:06,360
If it weren't for the Japanese advertisers and the peep shows, I don't know where we'd be.

340
00:27:07,080 --> 00:27:12,240
We never did go so far as to say space available on those boards, but almost.

341
00:27:12,241 --> 00:27:21,200
One notable exception was the spectacolor sign, a programmable message sign that anchored the Times Tower for 15 years.

342
00:27:21,920 --> 00:27:27,920
It became a very populist sign in Times Square because it was accessible to people.

343
00:27:28,200 --> 00:27:35,240
In addition to multinational or big advertisers that were able to advertise their products or services,

344
00:27:35,241 --> 00:27:42,460
we allowed people to wish one another a happy birthday or will you marry me or welcome to New York.

345
00:27:42,580 --> 00:27:46,760
And we had many, many requests and we fulfilled many, many of them.

346
00:27:47,000 --> 00:27:50,400
And I hope all of those marriages are still successful and thriving.

347
00:27:51,900 --> 00:27:58,440
A new generation of high-tech signs would soon bring a heightened level of excitement and spectacle.

348
00:27:59,300 --> 00:28:02,860
Up next, inside one of the most complex signs in the world,

349
00:28:03,300 --> 00:28:06,080
a 40-foot Coke bottle above Times Square.

350
00:28:07,540 --> 00:28:11,360
26 million tourists pour through Times Square every year.

351
00:28:11,980 --> 00:28:14,260
Times Square will return on Modern Marvels.

352
00:28:15,480 --> 00:28:19,200
We now return to Times Square on Modern Marvels.

353
00:28:19,201 --> 00:28:29,680
Attention getting signs and displays are a big part of the dizzying ambience and street show of Times Square.

354
00:28:30,180 --> 00:28:35,000
A show that has become very big budget and very high tech in the 21st century.

355
00:28:36,220 --> 00:28:42,120
For sheer complexity of design, nothing can compare with a $3 million Coke sign

356
00:28:42,121 --> 00:28:48,820
that was constructed, transported and installed on the northern face of Times Square in 1991.

357
00:28:49,200 --> 00:28:56,200
Coca-Cola sign is definitely the world's smartest sign.

358
00:28:56,201 --> 00:29:02,200
It has six different computer systems all talking to each other.

359
00:29:02,201 --> 00:29:07,200
In order to get all of these effects, we had to hire a robotics company,

360
00:29:07,201 --> 00:29:11,200
the company that makes the arms for the space shuttles.

361
00:29:11,201 --> 00:29:14,200
We knew that we wanted the bottle to fill and empty,

362
00:29:14,201 --> 00:29:18,200
but we had no idea how we were going to achieve this.

363
00:29:18,201 --> 00:29:19,924
We did experiments with liquid.

364
00:29:19,925 --> 00:29:25,040
We thought of having a tank in the building and pumping the liquid in and out of the bottle.

365
00:29:25,600 --> 00:29:29,060
And then we were concerned about freezing and leakage and whatnot.

366
00:29:29,560 --> 00:29:32,240
So finally we came up with this fiber optics idea.

367
00:29:32,840 --> 00:29:36,960
This was the first large-scale use of fiber optics in an outdoor display.

368
00:29:37,760 --> 00:29:44,780
The special challenge in the Coke sign was creating realistic color transitions from Coke brown to Coke bottle green,

369
00:29:44,781 --> 00:29:48,260
and vice versa as the bottle empties and fills.

370
00:29:49,400 --> 00:29:53,300
The only color you cannot get in light is brown.

371
00:29:54,000 --> 00:29:57,060
There's no brown neon or incandescent lamps.

372
00:29:57,580 --> 00:30:00,260
And here the Coca-Cola bottle, when it's full,

373
00:30:00,400 --> 00:30:03,580
has this very distinctive Coca-Cola color.

374
00:30:06,080 --> 00:30:07,620
We're at the Coke sign on Times Square.

375
00:30:08,420 --> 00:30:10,840
In here in the bottle we have 70,000 holes

376
00:30:10,841 --> 00:30:14,000
and about 60 miles of fiber optic cable.

377
00:30:14,001 --> 00:30:18,480
We have 70 light sources like this, which 56 of them are movable,

378
00:30:18,720 --> 00:30:20,900
and it lights up the fiber optic for the Coke bottle.

379
00:30:22,260 --> 00:30:25,260
Artcraft Strauss, which designed and installed the sign,

380
00:30:25,740 --> 00:30:30,560
achieved the desired effect by embedding the inside of the Coke bottle with fiber optic cables.

381
00:30:31,400 --> 00:30:34,580
What we arranged was a series of illuminators controlled by a computer,

382
00:30:34,760 --> 00:30:36,320
which again takes orders from the host computer,

383
00:30:36,760 --> 00:30:40,440
but the computer would then have these gels that would,

384
00:30:40,441 --> 00:30:43,460
at a signal the gels would go from brown to green.

385
00:30:44,000 --> 00:30:49,440
in a series starting at the top and there were 56 of these uh illuminated sources and they would go

386
00:30:49,441 --> 00:30:53,440
down down down down down until we got to the bottom of the bottle and the brown seemingly

387
00:30:53,441 --> 00:31:00,480
turned green the physical movement of the coke sign is also quite impressive especially that

388
00:31:00,481 --> 00:31:06,240
of the periactoids 20-foot triangular wedges that rotate to display different drinks

389
00:31:07,120 --> 00:31:13,600
there have been tri-vision signs before with three pictures that rotate but nobody ever tried

390
00:31:13,601 --> 00:31:15,038
covering one with neon.

391
00:31:15,039 --> 00:31:19,040
The tolerance on these things is less than an eighth of an inch.

392
00:31:19,840 --> 00:31:24,000
If they hit each other, there'll be glass flying all over the place, but if they don't come really,

393
00:31:24,001 --> 00:31:26,975
really close, it won't form a solid picture.

394
00:31:26,976 --> 00:31:29,680
So the engineering was just magnificent.

395
00:31:33,440 --> 00:31:38,160
The Koch sign may have the most sophisticated moving parts on Times Square,

396
00:31:38,161 --> 00:31:42,240
but the technology behind many of the other displays is also fascinating.

397
00:31:43,600 --> 00:31:50,600
Gigantic computerized printer drums, similar to those in home printers, only much, much larger,

398
00:31:50,601 --> 00:31:57,600
can spin out every shade of color at 300 dots per inch, creating colossal, photorealistic images

399
00:31:57,601 --> 00:32:02,600
that gaze across the square like advertising gods.

400
00:32:02,601 --> 00:32:08,600
Jumbo TV screens like this one from Sony are made up of hundreds of thousands of pixels,

401
00:32:08,601 --> 00:32:12,600
half-inch light sources that can glow green, blue, and red.

402
00:32:12,601 --> 00:32:18,440
A computer mixes the primary colors as needed to create thousands of shadings that appear on the screen.

403
00:32:20,480 --> 00:32:30,300
This curving NASDAQ marvel, the largest screen in the world, covers thousands of square feet and features office windows as part of its innovative design.

404
00:32:34,940 --> 00:32:39,080
Three-dimensional signs are still an important part of the Times Square mix.

405
00:32:39,700 --> 00:32:43,480
This six-foot light switch earned a place in the Guinness Book of World Records.

406
00:32:47,980 --> 00:32:54,660
And this perfectly detailed 110-foot Concorde caused a sensation when it suddenly appeared on its perch,

407
00:32:55,320 --> 00:32:57,260
prompting rumors that it could actually fly.

408
00:32:58,580 --> 00:33:01,860
But there's more to assign than assembling its parts and putting it up.

409
00:33:02,600 --> 00:33:04,660
Maintenance is an ongoing challenge.

410
00:33:04,661 --> 00:33:09,500
Confetti is one of our major problems, and it wreaks havoc in our signs,

411
00:33:09,501 --> 00:33:13,500
because most of our signs have to be ventilated or air-cooled,

412
00:33:13,501 --> 00:33:16,300
and it is sucked right inside of the signs.

413
00:33:16,301 --> 00:33:21,020
And for weeks after the events, we're cleaning up confetti.

414
00:33:22,700 --> 00:33:24,900
Pigeons are, of course, another problem.

415
00:33:25,300 --> 00:33:28,380
You know, if they drop on light bulbs, the light bulbs don't look that great.

416
00:33:28,800 --> 00:33:31,380
If they drop on a picture, you know,

417
00:33:31,381 --> 00:33:34,660
They always manage to drop on the most critical part of the picture.

418
00:33:34,920 --> 00:33:36,640
So we have to get up and clean it off.

419
00:33:38,740 --> 00:33:41,740
As expensive as these signs are to design and maintain,

420
00:33:42,260 --> 00:33:44,580
the real cost here is in leasing space.

421
00:33:45,980 --> 00:33:50,780
NASDAQ pays $3.7 million a year for the right to display its big screen.

422
00:33:53,220 --> 00:33:56,820
And the old times tower, which is unoccupied above the third floor,

423
00:33:57,540 --> 00:34:00,480
generates more than $10 million a year in rentals.

424
00:34:00,481 --> 00:34:02,700
as a prompt for signs.

425
00:34:04,080 --> 00:34:09,060
Economically, that translates to pennies per thousand exposures.

426
00:34:09,540 --> 00:34:11,360
So it's very, very economical.

427
00:34:11,840 --> 00:34:15,580
And it's there 24 hours a day, seven days a week.

428
00:34:16,240 --> 00:34:19,980
People pass by it over and over and they see pictures of it over and over.

429
00:34:20,460 --> 00:34:24,460
So if you compare the cost of a minute on the Super Bowl,

430
00:34:25,140 --> 00:34:29,420
you see that the spectacular is much more bang for the buck.

431
00:34:30,480 --> 00:34:38,560
With limitless high-tech resources, the future of Times Square advertising promises to be nothing short of mind-boggling.

432
00:34:39,820 --> 00:34:46,400
Holograms, interactivity, all of these things are right on the horizon.

433
00:34:47,460 --> 00:34:56,960
The time when a 70-foot tall celebrity will step right off the roof, walk right up to you and say,

434
00:34:56,961 --> 00:35:01,980
Hello, I see your insurance policy is about to expire. Wouldn't you like to buy?

435
00:35:03,600 --> 00:35:04,720
That'll be soon.

436
00:35:05,720 --> 00:35:11,360
That may seem hard to imagine, but stranger things have already happened in the world's crossroads.

437
00:35:11,940 --> 00:35:16,200
Coming up, the improbable marriage between Walt Disney and Times Square.

438
00:35:18,060 --> 00:35:24,320
Times Square is the only place in America with zoning laws that require developers to put up bright signs.

439
00:35:25,140 --> 00:35:27,420
Times Square will return on Modern Marvels.

440
00:35:28,520 --> 00:35:32,160
We now return to Times Square on Modern Marvels.

441
00:35:36,300 --> 00:35:40,380
Times Square today is as bright and as prosperous as it's ever been.

442
00:35:42,280 --> 00:35:44,840
It's hard to believe that just a few years ago,

443
00:35:44,841 --> 00:35:49,300
this imposing mix of upscale retailers, restaurants, and corporate logos

444
00:35:49,301 --> 00:35:52,440
was both down and out and down and dirty.

445
00:35:54,320 --> 00:36:00,360
In its own way, the Times Square of the 1970s and 80s added to the area's myth and lore.

446
00:36:00,880 --> 00:36:07,240
You know, I think if people want a picture of Times Square, they should go rent the movie Taxi Driver

447
00:36:07,241 --> 00:36:15,740
and take a look at the two main sequences in which Robert De Niro was driving in his taxi along Times Square of the 1970s.

448
00:36:16,340 --> 00:36:17,660
And what do you see there?

449
00:36:17,661 --> 00:36:21,980
You see prostitutes, drug dealers, violent criminals.

450
00:36:24,180 --> 00:36:25,360
That's what it was like.

451
00:36:28,500 --> 00:36:30,700
42nd Street between 7th and 8th Avenue.

452
00:36:31,360 --> 00:36:33,820
The most popular street in the world, I think.

453
00:36:34,580 --> 00:36:38,160
This year in the 70s, every one of these movie theaters were X-rated.

454
00:36:39,120 --> 00:36:44,540
We had drug dealers, prostitutes, pimps, everybody on the street.

455
00:36:44,541 --> 00:36:53,540
Undercover Vice Officer Sam Salerno patrolled Times Square from the backseat of a cab between 1972 and 1982.

456
00:36:53,541 --> 00:36:59,540
On a typical Saturday night, you'd have a tremendous amount of people in the city.

457
00:36:59,541 --> 00:37:04,540
Everybody would be partying. Some people would be here to have a good time,

458
00:37:04,541 --> 00:37:08,540
and some people would be here to take advantage of people having a good time.

459
00:37:08,541 --> 00:37:12,540
And you would have to be able to find them before they found the victim.

460
00:37:12,541 --> 00:37:18,540
alcohol, drugs would influence people, and there'd be fights, knifings, shootings,

461
00:37:19,420 --> 00:37:22,480
and it'd be going on right here in the street.

462
00:37:23,500 --> 00:37:26,420
The deteriorating street scene affected everyone,

463
00:37:26,900 --> 00:37:30,320
including such pillars of Times Square as the New York Times.

464
00:37:30,980 --> 00:37:35,640
We had a truck driver sitting in his truck while the newspapers were being loaded into the back,

465
00:37:35,641 --> 00:37:39,400
into the bays, shot right in his truck, just sitting there.

466
00:37:39,401 --> 00:37:45,400
And I remember that at its lowest point, there were some journalists who we couldn't hire

467
00:37:45,401 --> 00:37:51,740
because they couldn't stomach the walk from the Port Authority to our front door.

468
00:37:51,880 --> 00:37:52,720
That's a block and a half.

469
00:37:53,740 --> 00:37:54,940
It was very bad.

470
00:37:55,880 --> 00:38:02,220
By the early 1980s, with New York on the verge of bankruptcy and Times Square a symbol of its decline,

471
00:38:03,040 --> 00:38:07,580
the city okayed a radical redevelopment plan that would have gotten rid of the sleaze,

472
00:38:07,581 --> 00:38:09,900
along with just about everything else.

473
00:38:10,940 --> 00:38:15,540
The proposal called for the construction of 27 50-story-plus high-rises,

474
00:38:16,200 --> 00:38:19,680
including a quartet that would have surrounded and dwarfed the Times Tower.

475
00:38:21,960 --> 00:38:25,900
Big signs and flashing lights would have been eliminated from Times Square.

476
00:38:27,480 --> 00:38:29,980
You were taking this lively area,

477
00:38:30,220 --> 00:38:34,380
which one writer described as a circus tent roof by the sky,

478
00:38:34,381 --> 00:38:41,440
and you were changing it into a very, very narrow canyon of very, very tall and dark buildings.

479
00:38:42,020 --> 00:38:44,220
Wall Street was exactly what everybody had in mind.

480
00:38:44,900 --> 00:38:49,340
The city was offering zoning incentives to get people to build bigger buildings

481
00:38:49,341 --> 00:38:53,320
and it would have been just like Wall Street or Park Avenue

482
00:38:53,321 --> 00:38:56,220
or any other office street in America after dark.

483
00:38:57,280 --> 00:39:01,120
Groups like the Municipal Arts Society led an emotional campaign

484
00:39:01,121 --> 00:39:03,560
to stop the city from demolishing Times Square.

485
00:39:04,380 --> 00:39:14,786
Among other things, they organized a blackout, pulling the plug on all the lights in the area to dramatize what the new Times Square would look like without flamboyant

486
00:39:14,787 --> 00:39:15,487
signs.

487
00:39:15,780 --> 00:39:18,900
That is no mean feat because there's not a single switch.

488
00:39:19,000 --> 00:39:24,380
We had to have about 11 electricians with stopwatches and we turned off the lights.

489
00:39:24,960 --> 00:39:28,760
And so to see what would this neighborhood look like if it was just an office district.

490
00:39:28,880 --> 00:39:30,420
And it was, of course, terrifying.

491
00:39:30,421 --> 00:39:36,880
It would have been dangerous and unsafe as well as dead in human terms.

492
00:39:37,620 --> 00:39:42,000
The city finally abandoned its high-rise plans in the late 1980s

493
00:39:42,001 --> 00:39:44,620
and came up with another vision for Times Square,

494
00:39:45,100 --> 00:39:50,320
one built around a new generation of bright lights, media icons, and shopping.

495
00:39:50,980 --> 00:39:53,220
The critical investment was Disney's.

496
00:39:54,280 --> 00:39:58,140
That was the critical turnaround came when the Disney company

497
00:39:58,141 --> 00:40:01,060
first decided to consider investing

498
00:40:01,061 --> 00:40:03,680
and then made their investment contingent

499
00:40:03,681 --> 00:40:05,060
on whether we could clean up the sex shops.

500
00:40:07,240 --> 00:40:08,540
Incredibly, Disney,

501
00:40:08,860 --> 00:40:12,240
the most conservative, family-oriented of entertainment companies,

502
00:40:12,800 --> 00:40:15,580
agreed to set up shop on 42nd Street,

503
00:40:16,160 --> 00:40:19,340
which had been the most porn-saturated street in the square.

504
00:40:20,240 --> 00:40:21,880
We walked out into this room and we announced

505
00:40:21,881 --> 00:40:24,200
that Disney was considering taking over

506
00:40:24,201 --> 00:40:26,440
a broken-down theater in Times Square,

507
00:40:26,441 --> 00:40:29,060
and they were going to put in, they were going to fix it

508
00:40:29,061 --> 00:40:33,080
and they were going to have live productions for families on Times Square.

509
00:40:33,640 --> 00:40:36,880
When that was announced, the group of reporters began laughing.

510
00:40:37,480 --> 00:40:40,960
The word families and Times Square seemed very, very strange.

511
00:40:41,360 --> 00:40:44,080
And when the press conference was over with, Mr. Eisner said to me

512
00:40:44,081 --> 00:40:47,200
something about how, you know, we were going to have to follow through

513
00:40:47,201 --> 00:40:49,560
on our promise to clean the place up.

514
00:40:49,660 --> 00:40:50,700
Otherwise, this wasn't going to work.

515
00:40:51,560 --> 00:40:54,860
Mayor Giuliani went after the peep shows and adult bookstores

516
00:40:54,861 --> 00:40:59,960
with controversial zeal, ramming through new zoning regulations that made it illegal for

517
00:40:59,961 --> 00:41:03,591
adult businesses to operate within 500 yards of each other.

518
00:41:03,592 --> 00:41:05,560
The new rules would have reduced

519
00:41:05,561 --> 00:41:10,218
the adult operators in Times Square from 47 to 4.

520
00:41:10,219 --> 00:41:14,020
In fact, a majority of the adult stores,

521
00:41:14,300 --> 00:41:18,960
operating under a law enabling them to stay in business so long as 60% of their merchandise

522
00:41:18,961 --> 00:41:23,960
wasn't porn, still operate on the margins of Times Square.

523
00:41:23,961 --> 00:41:28,300
But once it was clear that porn was out of the limelight and that Mickey and Minnie were

524
00:41:28,301 --> 00:41:35,300
moving in, other media and retail giants lined up to grab a stake in the new Times Square.

525
00:41:35,301 --> 00:41:40,700
The Disneyfication of the area, as it is often described, has brought prosperity and safety

526
00:41:40,701 --> 00:41:42,900
to the famed bowtie.

527
00:41:42,901 --> 00:41:47,360
One largely positive side effect of the new Times Square is the fact that a number of

528
00:41:47,361 --> 00:41:52,460
historic theaters have been renovated and saved, although here too the corporate helping

529
00:41:52,461 --> 00:41:53,999
hand is highly visible.

530
00:41:54,000 --> 00:41:58,080
Disney restored the new Amsterdam Theater, in which mushrooms

531
00:41:58,081 --> 00:42:01,439
had literally been sprouting on the orchestra floor.

532
00:42:01,440 --> 00:42:03,700
The theater is now used exclusively

533
00:42:03,701 --> 00:42:05,422
for Disney productions.

534
00:42:05,423 --> 00:42:10,140
Across the street, the new Selwyn Theater has been brought back

535
00:42:10,141 --> 00:42:18,060
to life courtesy of american airlines which renamed the venue in honor of itself and the once

536
00:42:18,061 --> 00:42:24,780
glorious empire theater home to a host of early broadway stars has also been preserved sort of

537
00:42:25,980 --> 00:42:30,860
the stage and auditorium have actually been gutted but the front of the theater is intact

538
00:42:30,861 --> 00:42:37,100
and now serves as a facade for a 25 theater multiplex somebody once said that broadway is

539
00:42:37,101 --> 00:42:39,885
the longest street with the shortest memory.

540
00:42:39,886 --> 00:42:42,480
They say there's a broken heart for every

541
00:42:42,481 --> 00:42:43,874
light on Broadway.

542
00:42:43,875 --> 00:42:49,140
And not to get weepy or sentimental or philosophical, but those days

543
00:42:49,141 --> 00:42:51,015
will probably never come again.

544
00:42:51,016 --> 00:42:54,280
The days of come on along and meet those dancing feet,

545
00:42:54,281 --> 00:42:57,817
rhythm, the tempo, the flavor, the beat of 42nd Street as it was.

546
00:42:57,818 --> 00:42:58,960
And it's a battle now

547
00:42:58,961 --> 00:43:01,569
of titans in the retail world.

548
00:43:01,570 --> 00:43:06,960
Warner Brothers store and 20th Century Fox store and Paramount

549
00:43:06,961 --> 00:43:11,380
Iron Man store and Disney store, it's a new way of life. You can't knock it.

550
00:43:12,840 --> 00:43:18,600
Maybe not, but it's important to note that something has been lost in the Times Square revival.

551
00:43:19,140 --> 00:43:22,520
That a bit of the essence of the old Times Square is fading.

552
00:43:23,660 --> 00:43:31,920
A wilder quality, a hint of danger, a richer street life, a riskier seductiveness and allure.

553
00:43:31,921 --> 00:43:35,080
the bright lights are brighter than ever

554
00:43:35,081 --> 00:43:37,600
but Times Square to quote one writer

555
00:43:37,601 --> 00:43:40,360
used to be more complex in its glitter

556
00:43:40,361 --> 00:43:43,640
that said

557
00:43:43,641 --> 00:43:46,940
there's no denying that Times Square has transformed itself

558
00:43:46,941 --> 00:43:48,180
dramatically

559
00:43:48,181 --> 00:43:50,620
pulled itself out of decline

560
00:43:50,621 --> 00:43:52,200
amped up the lights

561
00:43:52,201 --> 00:43:53,700
brought back the tourists

562
00:43:53,701 --> 00:43:55,760
resurrected the signs

563
00:43:55,761 --> 00:43:57,520
and remade itself

564
00:43:57,521 --> 00:44:00,440
into an iconic media and entertainment address

565
00:44:00,441 --> 00:44:04,500
that is coveted by the biggest companies in the world.

